{"parentUuid":"0dda0ccc-8546-4b81-8ead-3b48197fe224","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"0cb0ffaf-0ce7-4ac2-82c7-317b2c960d87","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"squishy-yawning-papert","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01EhHbGwsQ1e1tE2UL3zUK23","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Google cluster data 2011 job_events schema columns event types\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cluster-data/ClusterData2011_2.md at master · google/cluster-data\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/cluster-data/blob/master/ClusterData2011_2.md\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis and Clustering of Workload in Google\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.01426\"},{\"title\":\"Google Cluster Trace Dataset Overview\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/google-cluster-trace-dataset\"},{\"title\":\"Google cluster-usage traces: format + schema\",\"url\":\"https://t1.daumcdn.net/cfile/tistory/26542633597592552C\"},{\"title\":\"Google Cluster Usage Traces | PDF | Computer Data Storage | Scheduling (Computing)\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/901116490/Google-Cluster-Usage-Traces\"},{\"title\":\"Google cluster-usage traces: format + schema Charles Reiss, John Wilkes\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Auday-Al-Dulaimy/post/Are-there-any-datasets-for-cloudSim/attachment/59d61de379197b807797be3e/AS:273823268573184@1442295962733/download/Google+cluster+usage+traces.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Analysis and Clustering of Workload in Google Cluster Trace based on Resource Usage\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270593953_Analysis_and_Clustering_of_Workload_in_Google_Cluster_Trace_based_on_Resource_Usage\"},{\"title\":\"First Steps Exploring the Google Cluster dataset with iPython | Mark Stillwell\",\"url\":\"https://stillwell.me/~mark/post/first-steps-exploring-the-google-cluster-dataset-with-ipython/\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Traces from a Google Data Center\",\"url\":\"https://hal.science/hal-01870216/file/analyzing-traces-google.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - googleapis/google-cloudevents: Types for CloudEvents issued by Google\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/googleapis/google-cloudevents\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is information about the Google cluster data 2011 job_events schema columns and event types:\n\n## Google Cluster Data 2011 - Job Events Schema\n\nEach dataset includes six principal CSV tables: machine events, machine attributes, job events, task events, task constraints, and task (instance) resource usage.\n\n### Event Types\n\nThere are basically two types of events: ones that affect the scheduling state (e.g., a job is submitted, or it gets scheduled and becomes runnable, or its resource requests are updated), and ones that reflect state changes of a task (e.g., the task exits).\n\nThe specific event types include:\n\n- **SUBMIT (0)**: A task or job became eligible for scheduling.\n- **SCHEDULE (1)**: A job or task was scheduled on a machine. (It may not start running immediately due to code-shipping time, etc.)\n- **FINISH (4)**: A task or job completed normally.(no content)There are several events encountered by job or a task during its lifetime. They may include events such as submission of a job, its scheduling, eviction on being rescheduled, failure, completion or an event where a job might be killed and many others.\n\n### Job Events Table Columns\n\nThis document outlines the format and schema for Google cluster-usage traces, detailing the organization of data related to jobs, tasks, and machines within a compute cell. It describes various techniques for data obfuscation, timestamping, and unique identifiers, as well as the structure of data tables for machine events, job events, and resource usage.\n\n### Time Handling\n\nA time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace window. Examples: machines that existed at the start of the trace; a task or job that was submitted (or has already been scheduled) before start of the the trace.\n\n### Dataset Details\n\nGoogle cluster trace is a dataset released by Google in May 2011 and contains cell information of about 29 days.(no content)The total size of the compressed trace is approximately 41GB.(no content)Each task has a priority, a small integer that is mapped here into a sorted set of values, with 0 as the lowest priority (least important). Tasks with larger priority numbers generally get preference for resources over tasks with smaller priority numbers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5b066609-1956-40a0-b073-01f945a6d77c","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:51:39.752Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"Google cluster data 2011 job_events schema columns event types","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_vrtx_01AQCYvEAuuwH2xH1T2vW1KY","content":[{"title":"cluster-data/ClusterData2011_2.md at master · google/cluster-data","url":"https://github.com/google/cluster-data/blob/master/ClusterData2011_2.md"},{"title":"Analysis and Clustering of Workload in Google","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.01426"},{"title":"Google Cluster Trace Dataset Overview","url":"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/google-cluster-trace-dataset"},{"title":"Google cluster-usage traces: format + schema","url":"https://t1.daumcdn.net/cfile/tistory/26542633597592552C"},{"title":"Google Cluster Usage Traces | PDF | Computer Data Storage | Scheduling (Computing)","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/901116490/Google-Cluster-Usage-Traces"},{"title":"Google cluster-usage traces: format + schema Charles Reiss, John Wilkes","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Auday-Al-Dulaimy/post/Are-there-any-datasets-for-cloudSim/attachment/59d61de379197b807797be3e/AS:273823268573184@1442295962733/download/Google+cluster+usage+traces.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Analysis and Clustering of Workload in Google Cluster Trace based on Resource Usage","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270593953_Analysis_and_Clustering_of_Workload_in_Google_Cluster_Trace_based_on_Resource_Usage"},{"title":"First Steps Exploring the Google Cluster dataset with iPython | Mark Stillwell","url":"https://stillwell.me/~mark/post/first-steps-exploring-the-google-cluster-dataset-with-ipython/"},{"title":"Analyzing Traces from a Google Data Center","url":"https://hal.science/hal-01870216/file/analyzing-traces-google.pdf"},{"title":"GitHub - googleapis/google-cloudevents: Types for CloudEvents issued by Google","url":"https://github.com/googleapis/google-cloudevents"}]},"Based on the search results, here is information about the Google cluster data 2011 job_events schema columns and event types:\n\n## Google Cluster Data 2011 - Job Events Schema\n\nEach dataset includes six principal CSV tables: machine events, machine attributes, job events, task events, task constraints, and task (instance) resource usage.\n\n### Event Types\n\nThere are basically two types of events: ones that affect the scheduling state (e.g., a job is submitted, or it gets scheduled and becomes runnable, or its resource requests are updated), and ones that reflect state changes of a task (e.g., the task exits).\n\nThe specific event types include:\n\n- **SUBMIT (0)**: A task or job became eligible for scheduling.\n- **SCHEDULE (1)**: A job or task was scheduled on a machine. (It may not start running immediately due to code-shipping time, etc.)\n- **FINISH (4)**: A task or job completed normally.(no content)There are several events encountered by job or a task during its lifetime. They may include events such as submission of a job, its scheduling, eviction on being rescheduled, failure, completion or an event where a job might be killed and many others.\n\n### Job Events Table Columns\n\nThis document outlines the format and schema for Google cluster-usage traces, detailing the organization of data related to jobs, tasks, and machines within a compute cell. It describes various techniques for data obfuscation, timestamping, and unique identifiers, as well as the structure of data tables for machine events, job events, and resource usage.\n\n### Time Handling\n\nA time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace window. Examples: machines that existed at the start of the trace; a task or job that was submitted (or has already been scheduled) before start of the the trace.\n\n### Dataset Details\n\nGoogle cluster trace is a dataset released by Google in May 2011 and contains cell information of about 29 days.(no content)The total size of the compressed trace is approximately 41GB.(no content)Each task has a priority, a small integer that is mapped here into a sorted set of values, with 0 as the lowest priority (least important). Tasks with larger priority numbers generally get preference for resources over tasks with smaller priority numbers."],"durationSeconds":20.077412182},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0dda0ccc-8546-4b81-8ead-3b48197fe224"}
